-- chem/freedual.cub — a fold's partition function bracketed by local objects, tested where the exact answer is known:
-- chem/selection.cub's HP model (6 residues on the square lattice, the first step fixed, 71 walks). At fugacity x per
-- H–H contact, Z_s(x) = Σ_walks x^{contacts} (so F = −ln Z; Z is bracketed, no logarithm taken). The contacts possible
-- are (0,3), (0,5), (1,4), (2,5): three span 3 steps of the chain and one spans all 5.
-- Above (a lower bound on F): the window relaxation at level k. A sequence of 5 steps is admitted when every k
-- consecutive steps are self-avoiding, and a contact (a, b) is weighed exactly when b − a ≤ k and by its largest value
-- (x when both residues are H) when it spans more than a window. Every true walk is admitted and weighs at most its
-- relaxed weight, so Z_k ≥ Z; the constraint and the weights are functions of windows of k steps, so Z_k is the
-- contraction of a transfer matrix on k − 1 steps (evaluated here by the direct sum over the 4⁴ step sequences, each
-- sequence's geometry tabulated once, `fd-recs`). Z_5 = Z. Windows nest, so Z_2 ≥ Z_3 ≥ Z_4 ≥ Z_5.
-- Below (an upper bound on F): the true walks confined to a strip of rows, an explicit restricted family (itself a
-- transfer matrix across the strip): rows 8–9 (two rows, L₂) and rows 7–9 (three rows, L₃), L₂ ≤ L₃ ≤ Z.
-- Proved by computation, every one of the 64 sequences, at x = 2 and x = 4 (`fd-chain-2`, `fd-chain-4`): Z_5 equals the
-- enumeration's Σ_c n_c x^{hh(s,c)} (chem/selection.cub's `sl-hist`), and L₂ ≤ L₃ ≤ Z ≤ Z_4 ≤ Z_3 ≤ Z_2.
